    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nbase16\n\n### Description\n\nWhile nvim-treesitter does export a lot of capture groups as built-in neovim groups, some are unaffected.\r\nA particularly relevant one is the `@symbol` capture group, which refers to atoms (the most common term).\r\nAtoms are essentially immutable strings with different comparison semantics, used in several languages such as Elixir, many lisps, and others.\r\nThey're very commonly used, and need to be differentiable from variables, but by default the highlight groups end up the same under Mini16.\r\nI've achieved this in my theme temporarily using `vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, '@symbol', { fg = palette.base0E })`, which roughly matches what non-compliant (since `@symbol` is the correct capture to use) grammars use.\r\nThe equivalent in `base16.lua` would be `hi('@symbol', { fg=p.base0E, bg=nil, attr=nil, sp=nil})`, which I would submit if I was sure that this is the desirable outcome.\r\nWhile `@symbol` is the specific one of note for me today, it may be interesting to set up highlight groups for all the treesitter groups (though it is also out of scope for today).",
